`,s.jsx(j,{items:[{q:"Derive Bayes' theorem from the definition of conditional probability in two lines.",a:"P(A and B) can be written two ways: P(A|B)P(B) and P(B|A)P(A). Set them equal and divide by P(B): P(A|B) = P(B|A)P(A)/P(B). That is the whole derivation."},{q:"Name each of the four pieces of Bayes' theorem and say which one people forget.",a:"Posterior P(A|B) — what you want. Likelihood P(B|A) — how well the hypothesis explains the evidence. Prior P(A) — the base rate. Evidence P(B) — the normaliser. The prior is the forgotten one, and ignoring it is exactly the base-rate fallacy."},{q:"A test is 99% accurate for a disease with 1-in-10,000 prevalence. Reason about P(disease | positive) without computing exactly.",a:"In 10,000 people: 1 true case, caught with near-certainty, versus about 1% of the 9,999 healthy people = roughly 100 false positives. So about 1 in 101 positives is real — under 1%. The healthy population is so much larger that its small error rate produces far more positives than the disease does."},{q:"P(A)=0.4, P(B)=0.5, P(A and B)=0.25. Independent?",a:"No. Independence needs P(A)P(B) = P(A and B), i.e. 0.20, but the joint is 0.25. B occurring makes A more likely than its base rate, so the two events carry information about each other."}]}),`
